e identified these  spikes you can then stop at that frame  and look at the bottom half of the  profiler window which displays detailed  information from the currently selected  profiler about the current framework  data  you can inspect script code certain  assets and resources that might be  slowing it down basically the profiler  is the first thing that you should look  at when looking to optimize your game's  performance  number two multiple cameras rendering  this might be specific to me but this  was the  biggest biggest problem that was  affecting performance  i had multiple cameras rendering my  scene which meant i had  multiplied the amount of draw calls that  were even needed 120  15.  now these cameras might be used for  things that you wouldn't even expect  would be hitting performance such as  reflections for materials or a separate  camera on your player for inspecting  certain layers however if these are  always on and never turned off when you  don't need them they are still rendering  and you are still losing performance  because of that i personally had a  security camera in one of my scenes and  it was rendering all of the time  regardless of whether the player was  even within that room or not to optimize  this i severely reduced the amount of  frames per second that the camera would  render having it only render the camera  when the specified amount of frames had  elapsed within the update of a script  that i'd put on the camera itself  and because this logic was running in  the update which means every frame i put  some checks in there first to check  whether the game object itself was  actually active and also to check  whether the player themselves were  actually facing the camera  using the dot product method proved  extremely useful when trying to up my  game's performance especially if the  objects themselves had scripts that had  logic running within the updates  i will actually put a link to a video  about how to use dot products in unity  in the description below it's really  useful check it out one other thing that  i did to improve performance was use  collision boxes which would turn the  camera  game object on or off depending on  whether the player had entered or exited  the collision area  number three use layers you can use  layers for ui ray cass or specifying  what assets of a scene a camera can  actually render alongside my security  camera i was also using some water for  my terrain which had a reflective  refractive material by default the  material was set to reflect all layers  of the scene which was greatly impacting  my performance so what i did was i just  duplicated the parts of the scene that i  needed the water to reflect  and then set them to their own layer so  that the player wouldn't see them no  other cameras would see them they'd seem  like they weren't there but they were  there specifically to be reflected by  the water  this really helped performance i used  this for every single camera in my scene  as well they only rendered what i needed  them to render and what was directly in  front of them occlusion also does help  with that though but more on that later  number four  optimize your models this is where i  really struggled because i'm not a  modeler  and uh  yeah i thought i would be doing mainly  coding and scripting which i enjoy doing  and then when i realized that i probably  also need to update  my models and make them more performant  i was like  but we did it so let me tell you how  multiple me's okay so  how i noticed that my scene and my  models were probably causing me an issue  was that i opened up these stats which  is at the top right of your editor  opening this will show your frame rate  so it's really good to have open anyway  but also it shows you your tries and  verts and my tries and rats were bloody  massive just like huge numbers and they  still are to be honest because i didn't  have time to and and the knowledge  to remodel everything um so t