 unity's website only game objects  sharing the same material can be batched  together therefore if you want to  achieve good batching you should aim to  share materials among as many different  game objects as possible we had done  this initially because we had set up the  whole environment within maya and then  just exported it all as one fbx which  then created unique instances for each  of the parts of the fbx that were  exported  this was to save time with setting up  the environment within unity itself  but now obviously i see the drawbacks to  that which is performance it would add  to the draw calls greatly because each  mesh would be seen as a totally unique  object instead of duplicates of one  another which they technically were so  to rectify this i used one instance of  the mesh and its material on all of its  duplicates i highly recommend that you  go and do some research into batching  because it's not quite as simple as one  draw call per model there are other  factors  that  come into play here such as per pixel  lighting shadows and things like that  there are two main types of batching and  that is dynamic batching which is for  small enough meshes that transforms  their vertices on the cpu groups many  similar vertices together and draws them  in one go and there's also static  batching which combines static unmoving  game objects into big meshes and renders  them in a faster way you can go into  your player settings to enable and  disable these they both do have their  drawbacks as well so please do research  more into those for example with static  batching i did find out that the entire  of the static batch meshes would be  rendered even if just one aspect of that  batch is seen by the camera which  means you could end up having more  vertices than you need six  occlusion culling told you we'd get  there another issue that i ran into by  having multiple meshes exported as one  fbx was occlusion  occlusion didn't work properly on those  meshes because if one part was seen by  the camera then the rest of it was also  going to be rendering and occlusion is  supposed to stop that occlusion is  supposed to only render what the player  can see within their camera viewport for  example the floor was one whole fbx and  that meant that the floor was constantly  being rendered the whole floor not just  sections of it bit by bit the whole  floor even if the player was on the  other side of the building and to be  fair the floor and the ceiling didn't  have too many vertices but we also had  the same issue with  multiple objects such as all of the  cupboards for one fbx so if one cupboard  was being rendered by the camera all of  the cupboards throughout the whole  building were being rendered  now i didn't have time to go in and fix  all of that myself however setting up  occlusion properly still very much  helped my game within the static drop  down of a game object you have two  options a clue d static and a cloudera  static  are game objects that can be culled and  i made everything  a clue d  apart from my monster because that kept  popping in an ounce of occlusion and i  needed the player to see that but  everything else was a 2ds and then you  had occluders  which are game objects that can  help with the culling it can include  everything behind it so this is great  for large objects such as walls or doors  that would never be opened locked doors  because one issue that i had was that  the train was constantly being rendered  because i hadn't set up my walls to be  occluders and when they were occluders  the terrain was no longer rendered when  the player could not see them through a  wall i also set up occlusion areas to  define view volumes in the occlusion  culling system to basically tell the  system where the player was most likely  to be for me this was very simple  because i was doing everything indoors  and i have a very  linear game play  so i could easily predict where the  player was going to be it's probably  going to be harder in  an open world game or something like  that at bake time unity generates higher  precision data within the view volumes  and at run time unity performs higher  precision calculations when the camera's  position is within a view volume and if  you don't specify a view volume unity  will create you one that just  encompasses all of your cloudies and  occluders and as you can imagine that  would be massive so you definitely do  help save on performance if you set  these up manually yourself but like i  say if you have more of an open world  you might just have to let unity do its  thing you can calculate the occlusion by  going to window and occlusion culling  usually the default settings are  perfectly fine but you might want to  play around with these just to get the  effect that you'd like again i'll put  more details on occlusion in the  description below number seven lights  baking like baking was always something  that i struggled to do within this game  because it changed dynamically from day  to night which as you can imagine  drastically changed the lighting within  the scene in hindsight now i should have  found a way to seamlessly transition to  a new level that would have definitely  helped performance and there are a  couple other things that i could do  which i will tell you right now so for  now i chose to use mixed lights and  baked lights mixed lights are the best  of both worlds combining real-time  lighting with some baked lighting as  well as long as global illumination is  enabled within your scene  and then baked lights cannot change  during runtime but they significantly  help to improve performance baked lights  reduce the cost of rendering shadows as  their lights is pre-calculated so i  baked some default lights into my scene  however it was a bit too light for the  nighttime transition  luckily i had already set up a post  process effect that was put on the  player when the night time happened and  i simply made this darker so i faked it  another possible avenue that i could  have gone down for this was to create  multiple different light maps and then i  could have switched out the light maps  at runtime because every time you bake  lights you get a light map and that's  what your game uses if you can get this  light map and then switch it out using a  script then  you can get different types of lights in  the same scene so  that could have been perfect but yeah i  didn't do that  i just think i ran out of patience to be  honest with you i also use light probes  to improve the quality of lighting on  dynamic objects within my scene light  probes are positions in the scene where  light is measured during the bake and  then this information can be used during  runtime to approximate indirect light  values from the nearest light probe when  it hits a dynamic game object overall  light baking is a very complex and  useful tool especially for improving  your games look and performance and i  will admit to not yet fully  understanding it however i will put in  the description a video about light maps  that i found very informative  number eight optimizing your code  ultimately every script is different and  there will be endless tips on how to  optimize your c sharp so i'll just focus  on the main points surprise surprise the  update we spoke about this a little bit  earlier but i definitely need to talk to  you about it in more detail every piece  of code in the update gets run every  single frame of your game hence why in  the security camera script that i showed  you earlier i had multiple checks before  running any logic that was put in my  update putting code into the update  without thinking about it is quite a  rookie move because when you first learn  about it it seems pretty logical i mean  if you have code in the update and it's  always running then it's easier to debug  whether it's working correctly and you  don't have to worry about whether it's  going to be triggered or not however  when optimizing you really need to ask  yourself does this logic need to be ran  every single frame if it's the character  movement probably yes  if it's a door opening and closing  probably not  and if it is not then think about  whether it can be put in its own  function that is triggered only when  it's needed simply put the less amount  of code in the update the more  performant your scripts will be  [Music]  for example i had a script that was  checking for certain actions to be done  by the player this would then increment  an integer variable called observant  this check was being done in a separate  script to the one that was managing the  player interactions and it was checking  whether the player was inspecting every  single frame whenever the player had  inspected an object it would then return  true and then increment plus one to the  observant integer number there was no  real need for this separate script to  have logic in its update to check  whether the player was interacting to  make this more performant i made the  observant variable global so that it  could be easily accessed by other  scripts and then i simply added the  increment logic into the interaction  script itself so that when a player  interacted with something it also then  incremented that global  observant integer variable no more  checks in the update were needed it  simply said player interacts update this  variable  and that was that it's all about  breaking down the logic and finding the  most direct route to get the results  that you need if you need code to run  frequently but not necessarily all of  the time then perhaps look into  co-routines i use these for short  lasting events and animations such as  lights flickering on and off and also ui  fading in and fading out in between  screen loading times however they do  have their own drawbacks as they can be  difficult to debug and they do create  garbage again i will put a link down to  a wonderful blog all about updates and  how to keep them performant in the  description below a function to look out  for this time is find game object i had  used this function a lot when i was  first starting out in game dev and i  thought i was really smart at the time  it saved me the time of having to  manually assign all of the relationships  between the assets because i could just  get the game to do it for me which is  great right  no  the game needs to be handling things  that should run the game not stuff that  you should be manually setting up  yourself come on you're lazy dane game  dev dane  i ended up using find game object in so  many of my scripts and i had to  slash it out just slash it out and  manually assign stuff because  that helped performance the  the game could then focus on running the  game instead of finding everything and  setting it up correctly which i should  have done beforehand when you call this  function the game has to manually loop  through all of the game objects that are  currently in the scene to find the one  that matches your name  that's  potentially  so so so many game objects and as you  can imagine if you have this happening  all at the same time over multiple  scripts it's definitely going to affect  performance to manually assign them you  can create a public variable and then  select the appropriate game object or  whatever that whatever that it is and  assign the correct one to it within the  inspector manually assigning though will  only work on game objects that are  already in the scene so if you're  planning on spawning something during  runtime then you might need to use this  or perhaps use more specific versions of  this i know there is fine game objects  with tag and things like that so  that will probably be better to use than  just the generic find game object and if  you're unsure whether you'll need them  then just put a check beforehand just  see whether the relationship has already  been established and if not then you can  find the game object i often did this  with things like my camera just in case  for some reason i would lose reference  to my camera because i was switching  cameras quite often so i would say yeah  if camera equals null  then find the main camera  lastly turn off scripts when they're not  needed you can do this like i explained  earlier with the collision collisions  are great well i don't that's how i do  it it's probably not a great way if you  know a better way than to turn off  things via collision let me know because  i've just had collisions everywhere  saying yeah the player is nowhere near  this thing so turn it off and don't do  all the things so  maybe you know a better way  tell me please  optimize your game object components  unity offers many components that define  the behaviors of the game object which  really makes our lives so much easier we  don't have to code every tiny detail  which is great especially for physics  it's great you simply select a game  object and add a new component in the  inspector window and ta-da your game  object now has physics ui collision or  so much more  so much variety however  some of these components are way more  performance expensive than others so do  make sure you're using the correct ones  for your needs don't do what i did and  just use mesh colliders on everything  because it's so much easier you're lazy  game death  oh man this is me berating my younger  self now isn't it mesh colliders have a  much higher performance impact than box  colliders or any other type of colliders  so really use them sparingly it seems  like the best collision to use because  it will create collision that perfectly  matches your geo however it will be  checking for collision against  potentially every single face of your  mesh  which is probably not needed  if you need to create collision that  does  match a certain shape maybe a rectangle  or something you can use multiple  collisions you can use two box  collisions to crea